#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
.githooks/post-commit -- auto-publish every commit to origin/<branch>.

Standing rule (AGENTS.md Rule 6, updated 2026-07-04): on the
`refine-coded-ai` branch, unattended pushes are sanctioned. The user
explicitly requested this after the Vite `define` chip landed, so
they no longer have to remember `git push` after every commit.

Why this hook exists (and stays trivial)
----------------------------------------
The previous post-commit hook on this repo tried to AMEND the
commit's content (bake the SHA into src/version-constants.js +
git amend) which created a chicken-and-egg and required a marker-
based anti-recursion + try/except cleanup. That hook was deleted
when the Vite `define` approach shipped (see SPEC.md §16) -- the
SHA now reads at config-load via `git rev-parse --short HEAD`,
honest by construction, no amend needed.

This NEW hook is simpler because it does NOT touch the commit's
content. It just calls `git push` -- which is additive (pushes any
upstream-missing commits + the current HEAD) and cannot re-fire the
source-side post-commit hook (only the receiving-side hooks fire,
and there are none on origin). Zero recursion risk -- NO MARKER
FILE NEEDED.

Failure handling
----------------
If git push returns non-zero (network down, auth expired, repo
removed, etc.):
- The commit STAYS committed locally (the commit happens BEFORE
  this hook fires; the hook is post-commit).
- The hook prints a stderr line + exits 1, so the user sees the
  failure on the terminal right after they typed git commit.
- The NEXT commit's hook re-attempts `git push`, which is additive
  -- it picks up the stray commit from last time + the new HEAD.

Idempotency
-----------
`git push` is idempotent on the receiving side (pushes only what
origin doesn't already have). So this hook can fire as many times
as the user commits without ever "double-pushing" anything.

Install
-------
git config core.hooksPath .githooks    # project-local, one-time
                                        # per fresh clone

Or use the convenience wrapper: `./scripts/install-hooks.sh`
"""
import subprocess
import sys


def main():
    try:
        subprocess.check_call(['git', 'push'])
        print('[post-commit] git push ok.')
    except subprocess.CalledProcessError as e:
        print(
            f'[post-commit] git push failed (exit {e.returncode}).\n'
            f'[post-commit] commit WAS COMMITTED LOCALLY but NOT PUSHED.\n'
            f'[post-commit] next commit will retry (git push is additive).',
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        sys.exit(1)


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
